{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/898360-wod-as-video-games-vs-tabletop-larp","id":898360,"title":"WoD as video games vs tabletop/larp?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-05-24T16:35:44-07:00","id":898360,"title":"WoD as video games vs tabletop/larp?","content":"Is there really a difference between video games and table top/larp games at a whole?<br /> <br /> For example, if Vampire: The Masquerade is ported into a video game like Redemption and Bloodlines was, is there any defining differences like for example how Vampires can learn out of clan disciplines, combat situations, etc?<br /> <br /> Since in the table top/larp game, disciplines are often very hard to learn when they're out of clan and plus I heard that even combat situations going in guns blazing will break the Masquerade alone, maybe not so much in video game form?<br /> <br /> Well what are the main differences between video games and tabletop/larp games especially with the case with any of the upcoming WoD games we might see from Obsidian/Paradox?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11735,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11735-spaceharrier72","user":"spaceharrier72","date":". 05-24-2016, 07:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11735-spaceharrier72","name":"spaceharrier72","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"47"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-24T19:33:02-07:00","id":898504,"title":"","content":"There's a scale here, since there's a bunch of video games which are as different from each other than they are from the traditional WoD games.<br /> <br /> The Hunter series of video games are a Gauntlet style shoot 'em up. The way Edges work has little to do with how they work in the TT/LARP rules, there's no social or investigatory aspect, and lots of issues (like going insane from increasing dedication to the Hunt) are abandoned to shoot more monsters. Not a knock against the games, but they're not deep because they're not meant to be.<br /> <br /> The VtM adaptations are a bit closer to the source material, but they still suffer from a greater default focus on combat, and getting people access to cool &quot;toys&quot; faster.<br /> <br /> LARP and TT and other styles (such as persistent chat environments) all have their own differences as well. LARPs tend to tamp down on violence over social and political stuff much harder. But a 20 person combat scene even with the LARP rules is still a pain, and it drags everything to a halt, where social stuff can flow much more easily for the format involved. LARPs also tend to have greater stress on the rarity of things (not just Disciplines) to maintain the fun of larger groups which have a greater amount of trouble creating a consensus around what's going to be fun.<br /> <br /> I've been in TT games that are as combat focused as the Hunter video games, and TT games run closer to LARP mentalities. It's probably the hardest to generalize about because it's whatever works for a handful of people that doesn't run into the technical limitations of video games, or the logistical limitations of LARPs/etc.<br /> <br /> What does this mean for any new games from Paradox? Who knows.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-25T13:54:18-07:00","id":899358,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>spaceharrier72</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/898360-wod-as-video-games-vs-tabletop-larp#post898360\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is there really a difference between video games and table top/larp games at a whole?</div> </div> </div> </div>For me there are quite a few..<br /> <br /> 1) A PC/console game can't anticipate all actions that could be taken, where as RPGs can, especially since often there is a line in then saying &quot;the DM is free to add/change rules to fit the situation&quot;..<br /> 2) Cheaper.. A pc/console game needs a console or PC to get played. So right there, you have at least 300 bucks of outlay. For RPGs, you need one main book (preferably 2), and maybe a sourcebook or two. Some dice, and paper and pens/pencils. All for less than 100.. <br /> 3) A pc game can't be played during a power outage. RPGs that are TT (or larp) can still go on..<br /> 4) Fellowship. Even with headsets in games like CoD, you generally don't have anywhere near the fellowship / camraderie that you do in a console/PC game as you would for TT games..<br /> 5) Socialization. We have all heard horror stories of someone who sat at home and wasted away cause of not properly eating, drinking, sleeping, going to the lav cause they just HAD to put in that 100 hr shift beating the newest PC game, all cause they imo socially isolate someone. Unlike a TT and LARP Rpg, where you HAVE to get out there and socialise..<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-27T04:50:55-07:00","id":900830,"title":"","content":"Well, the socialization aspect is kind of a non-issue nowadays. You can easily go onto roll20 and find a group of people to play with over the internet, and a lot of the time it's far easier to do so than it is to put together a group IRL.<br /> <br /> But on topic there is the strict technical limitations of video games to take into consideration when comparing them to TT. A videogame that's made today or in the foreseeable future will never be able to capture the freeform nature of a TT game, and that's the essence of what makes TT so compelling to me.<br /> That isn't to say that TT games will always be superior, videogames will be able to present a visual medium that you can't get otherwise, and you don't have to have a group of people to play them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4003-kristviljan","name":"Kristviljan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4003_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"110"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}